{# /root/.my.cnf — credentials for the cron-backed mysqldump ONLY.

   ⚠⚠ THERE IS DELIBERATELY NO [client] SECTION. DO NOT ADD ONE.

   The mysql client reads [client] and [mysql]; mysqldump reads [client] and
   [mysqldump]. While a [client] section existed here, every `mysql` command run
   AS ROOT on this droplet silently authenticated as the low-privilege backup
   user instead of root@localhost.

   That was not cosmetic. Read-only queries all succeeded, so diagnostics looked
   fine, and only MUTATING commands failed -- with
   `ERROR 1227 ... you need SUPER or SYSTEM_VARIABLES_ADMIN`. Measured
   2026-08-18: it broke `SET GLOBAL innodb_fast_shutdown = 0`, the step
   MYSQL-84-CUTOVER.md marks REQUIRED before an in-place upgrade. dpkg then
   stops mysqld with the default FAST shutdown and the data-dictionary upgrade
   begins from a non-clean state -- while the operator believes they followed
   the runbook, because the failure is one error line in a wall of output.

   Verified: with [mysqldump] alone, mysqldump still picks these credentials up
   and `mysql` falls through to root@localhost via auth_socket.

   root@localhost stays on the socket-auth plugin and is used via `sudo mysql`
   for ad-hoc maintenance. #}
[mysqldump]
user = {{ mysql_backup_user }}
password = "{{ mysql_backup_password }}"
